    id: "finance-02",
    category: "finance-business",
    icon: "&#x1F3A4;",
    title: "Investor Pitch Deck",
    description: "Create a compelling investor pitch deck that tells a clear story and drives investment decisions.",
    prompt: `Create an investor pitch deck following the Sequoia/Y Combinator format:\n\nSlide 1 — Title:\n- Company name and logo\n- One-line description (under 10 words)\n- Presenter name and contact\n\nSlide 2 — Problem:\n- The pain point in one sentence\n- Who has this problem and how many?\n- Current solutions and why they fail\n\nSlide 3 — Solution:\n- How you solve the problem\n- Core value proposition in 1 sentence\n- Product screenshot or demo GIF (show, don't tell)\n\nSlide 4 — Why Now:\n- Market timing: what changed that makes this possible now?\n- Technology shift, regulatory change, consumer behavior change\n- "Why hasn't this been built before?"\n\nSlide 5 — Market Size:\n- TAM, SAM, SOM with clear methodology\n- Bottom-up calculation preferred over top-down\n- Growth rate of the market\n\nSlide 6 — Product:\n- 3-5 core features with screenshots\n- What makes it 10x better (not just incrementally better)\n- Defensibility: network effects, data moat, IP, switching costs\n\nSlide 7 — Traction:\n- Key metrics chart (revenue, users, engagement — whatever matters most)\n- Growth rate (MoM or YoY)\n- Notable customers or partnerships (logos)\n- Unit economics: CAC, LTV, payback period, gross margin\n\nSlide 8 — Business Model:\n- How you make money\n- Pricing tiers with target customer per tier\n- Revenue per customer (actual or projected)\n- Path to $100M ARR\n\nSlide 9 — Competition:\n- 2x2 matrix or feature comparison (not just a list of names)\n- Your unfair advantage\n- "Why we win" (not "we have better features")\n\nSlide 10 — Go-to-Market:\n- Primary customer acquisition channel\n- CAC and how it decreases over time\n- Distribution strategy and partnerships\n\nSlide 11 — Team:\n- Founders: name, role, 1-line bio (relevant experience only)\n- Key advisors and investors (if notable)\n- Key hires planned\n- Why this team? (founder-market fit)\n\nSlide 12 — Financials:\n- 3-year revenue projection (simple, believable)\n- Key assumptions\n- Burn rate and runway\n- Path to profitability\n\nSlide 13 — The Ask:\n- How much you're raising\n- Instrument (priced round, SAFE, etc.)\n- Use of funds (pie chart or bar chart)\n- Milestones this round achieves\n\nAppendix slides (not in main deck, ready for Q&A):\n- Detailed customer case studies\n- Technical architecture deep dive\n- Cohort retention data\n- Detailed financial model\n- Competitive deep dives\n- Customer testimonials\n\nDesign principles:\n- 15-20 words max per slide (exceptions for product screenshots)\n- One key takeaway per slide\n- Consistent font, color scheme, visual style\n- High-quality images/screenshots (no clip art, no cheesy stock photos)\n- Charts clearly labeled with data sources\n- Slide count: 13 main + 5-8 appendix\n\nProvide the complete deck outline with speaker notes for each slide (what to SAY, not just what's on the slide).`,
