    prompt: `Build an internationalization (i18n) system for a [framework, e.g., React, Next.js, Vue]:\n\nTranslation Management:\n- JSON or YAML translation files organized by locale: en.json, es.json, fr.json, etc.\n- Nested key structure: "home.hero.title", "home.hero.subtitle"\n- Interpolation: "Hello, {{name}}!" with variable substitution\n- Pluralization: \"{{count}} item\" vs \"{{count}} items\" with language-specific rules\n- Formatting: dates, numbers, currencies per locale (use Intl API)\n\nLocale Detection:\n- Priority order: URL path (/en/about) > cookie > browser Accept-Language header > default\n- Auto-redirect on first visit based on browser language\n- Language switcher component with visual flag or language name\n- Persist user choice in cookie or localStorage\n\nRTL (Right-to-Left) Support:\n- CSS logical properties: margin-inline-start instead of margin-left\n- Direction-aware utility classes\n- Flip icons and arrows for RTL\n- Test with Arabic, Hebrew, Persian\n\nTranslation Workflow:\n- Extract: scan codebase for translation keys (i18next-parser or similar)\n- Translate: send to translators (or use Lokalise, Crowdin, Phrase)\n- Load: lazy-load translations per page/chunk to avoid large bundles\n- Fallback: show key name if translation missing (in dev), key in default locale (in prod)\n\nPerformance:\n- Split translations by namespace (common, auth, dashboard) — load only what's needed\n- Preload critical translations (navigation, error messages)\n- Cache translation bundles in Service Worker\n\nTesting:\n- Test all supported locales\n- Visual regression tests for layout at different text lengths (German is 30% longer than English)\n- Test RTL layout for supported RTL languages\n- Accessibility: screen reader language pronunciation\n\nProvide the complete setup with react-i18next or vue-i18n, locale files, and a language switcher component.`,
